<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Long Walk of the Navajo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Long Walk of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> was the forced removal of the Navajo people by the US Army in 1864 from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosque-redondo">Bosque Redondo</a> reservation. Some 9,000 people were marched about 480 km on foot in many groups, and hundreds died on the way. Their return in 1868 is remembered as a turning point in Navajo history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/long-walk-of-the-navajo.jpg" alt="Long Walk of the Navajo" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of the United States · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navajo_on_Long_Walk.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
